When Ines Calloway crossed the start line of her first hundred-mile race, she was wearing borrowed shoes and carrying a cheese sandwich. Around her, other runners bristled with poles, head torches and tiny bottles of gel. By the time she finished, thirty-one hours later, she had cried twice, eaten half a cold pizza from a stranger’s car boot, and decided that nothing in her life had ever been quite so sensible.

{{41}}

Nobody did. A colleague printed out an entry form, another lent her the shoes, and a retired runner from the village offered the only piece of advice she would later describe as essential. Slow down, he said, and then slow down again.

{{42}}

By midnight, the joy had gone. Her legs had turned to wood, her head torch was fading, and her stomach refused everything except flat lemonade.

{{43}}

A man called Piotr, whom she never saw again after dawn, walked beside her for six miles and talked about his allotment. She remembers almost nothing he said, only that it worked.

{{44}}

Even so, the final ten miles were hers alone. Daylight came up grey over the moor, and every step felt like an argument with her own body.

{{45}}

Her pupils now follow her races on a map pinned to the classroom wall, moving a small paper runner along the route each evening.

{{46}}
